// Practice quiz: Test doubles, fakes and stubs
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/testing-and-java-review/test-doubles-fakes-and-stubs

::o1 what a fake is::Which of these is a fake? {
=A simple working implementation written for tests, such as a store that keeps data in a List in memory#Correct. A fake really works, just in a simplified way, so it runs fast and needs no setup.
~A class that always returns the same fixed answer and has no logic#Not quite. That is a stub; it answers but does not remember anything.
~The production database, started fresh for each test#Not quite. That is the real dependency, used in an integration test.
~An object JUnit builds from an interface on its own#Not quite. JUnit does not build test doubles; you write a fake by hand.
}

::o1 remember then ask::An OrderService saves an order and later asks whether that order exists. Why does a fake fit this test better than a stub? {
=The fake remembers what was saved, so the later question gets the right answer#Correct. Saving and then asking needs a double with real, simple behavior.
~A stub cannot implement an interface#Not quite. A stub implements the same interface; it just returns canned answers.
~A fake connects to a real database, so it is more realistic#Not quite. A fake keeps data in memory and needs no database.
~A stub would stop the test from compiling#Not quite. It would compile; it would just give the same answer whatever was saved.
}

::o2 a fixed hour::GreetingService returns Good morning when clock.hour() is below 12, otherwise Good afternoon. A test passes in a stub Clock whose hour() always returns 20. What does the test gain? {
=The same Good afternoon result whenever the test runs, at any real time of day#Correct. The stub gives a canned hour, so the result no longer depends on the build server's clock.
~The test now reads the real clock, but only in the evening#Not quite. GreetingService asks the stub, not the system clock.
~GreetingService ignores the stub and uses the system time#Not quite. The service only calls the Clock it was given.
~The test can only run in the evening#Not quite. The hour is fixed by the stub, so the test passes at any time.
}

::o2 inside a stub::What belongs inside a stub's method? {
=A fixed, canned return value chosen for the test#Correct. A stub has no real logic; it just answers.
~A copy of the production logic#Not quite. Copying logic into a double would repeat any bug and add nothing.
~A call to the real dependency#Not quite. The point of the stub is to replace the real dependency.
~An assertion that checks how the stub was called#Not quite. A stub only answers; the test's own assertions check the result.
}

::o3 new inside the class::ReportService creates its own EmailSender with new inside its constructor. Why is it hard to unit test? {
=A test has no way to pass in a stand-in, so the real sender always runs#Correct. A class that creates its own dependency gives the test nothing to swap.
~Java constructors are not allowed to call new#Not quite. They can; the problem is that the test cannot choose what is created.
~JUnit cannot test a class that has a constructor#Not quite. JUnit tests classes with constructors all the time.
~It is not hard, because JUnit replaces every new call with a double#Not quite. JUnit does not replace dependencies; the test has to pass one in.
}

::o3 making it testable::Which change lets a test give ReportService a fake EmailSender? {
=Make EmailSender an interface and have ReportService receive it as a constructor parameter#Correct. The service then calls only the interface, so the test chooses the implementation.
~Make the EmailSender field private#Not quite. Private fields still run; the test still cannot replace the sender.
~Make the method that sends the report static#Not quite. A static method still creates or calls the real sender itself.
~Move the new call into another method of the same class#Not quite. The class still creates its own sender, so nothing can be swapped in.
}
